═══════════ railway connect --help ═══════════
Connect to a database's shell (psql for Postgres, mongosh for MongoDB, etc.)

Usage: railway connect [OPTIONS] [SERVICE_NAME]

Arguments:
  [SERVICE_NAME]  The name of the database to connect to

Options:
  -e, --environment <ENVIRONMENT>  Environment to pull variables from (defaults to linked environment)
  -p, --project <PROJECT_ID>       Project ID to use (defaults to linked project)
      --ssh                        Tunnel to the database over SSH instead of a public TCP proxy. Works without a public domain; auto-enabled when the service has no public proxy URL
      --no-ssh                     Force the public TCP proxy path and never fall back to SSH
      --tunnel-only                Open the local tunnel without launching a database client. Prints connection details for external tools (TablePlus, DBeaver, pgAdmin, ...) and holds the tunnel open until Ctrl+C. Implies --ssh
  -P, --port <PORT>                Local port to bind for the SSH tunnel (defaults to an ephemeral port)
  -h, --help                       Print help
  -V, --version                    Print version

Examples:

  railway connect postgres
  railway connect redis --environment production
  railway connect postgres --tunnel-only

Automation notes:
  Non-interactive runs must pass the database service name.
  The local database client must be installed before connecting.
  --tunnel-only skips the client and just opens the tunnel, for pointing
  external tools (TablePlus, DBeaver, pgAdmin, ...) at a private database.


